# Secure Private Container Registry with Image Lifecycle & Retention Policies
### Using Amazon ECR, IAM, and Docker

**Role:** DevOps Engineer
**Objective:** Design and configure a secure container registry system with access control, lifecycle rules, and automated image cleanup.

> **Note:** All AWS Account IDs and ARNs in this document are replaced with `<YOUR_ACCOUNT_ID>` placeholders. Replace them with your own account ID when reusing these policies.

---

## 1. Problem Statement

The company was storing Docker images in **public repositories** with no control, causing:

- **Security risk** — anyone on the internet could pull the images; accidental secrets baked into an image would be exposed.
- **Storage bloat** — old, unused image versions were never cleaned up, driving up storage cost.
- **No lifecycle management** — there was no automated way to retire outdated versions.

**Goal:** Move to a **private** registry (Amazon ECR) with IAM-controlled access and an automatic cleanup policy, so old images expire on their own and only authorized identities can pull/push.

---

## 2. Architecture

```
┌───────────────────────┐        docker push/pull        ┌─────────────────────────────┐
│   EC2 Instance          │ ─────────────────────────────> │   Amazon ECR (Private)        │
│   (Docker host)         │                                  │   Repository: my-app-repo     │
│   IAM Role attached:    │ <───────────────────────────── │                                │
│   EC2-ECR-Access-Role   │                                  │  Images: v1, v2, v3, v4,      │
└───────────────────────┘                                  │  latest                        │
                                                              │  + Lifecycle Policy            │
                                                              │  + Repository Policy           │
                                                              └─────────────────────────────┘
                                                                          ▲
                                                                          │ ecr:DescribeRepositories (DENIED)
                                                              ┌─────────────────────────────┐
                                                              │  IAM User: test-user          │
                                                              │  (no ECR permissions)         │
                                                              └─────────────────────────────┘
```

---

## 3. Implementation Steps

### Step 1 — EC2 Docker Host Setup
1. Launched an Amazon Linux EC2 instance (`docker-ecr-host`).
2. Attached an IAM Role (`EC2-ECR-Access-Role`) with `AmazonEC2ContainerRegistryFullAccess`, so the instance can authenticate to ECR without storing static credentials.
3. Installed Docker:
   ```bash
   sudo yum install docker -y
   sudo systemctl start docker
   sudo systemctl enable docker
   sudo usermod -aG docker ec2-user
   ```

### Step 2 — Private ECR Repository
1. Created a private repository: **`my-app-repo`**
2. Settings used:
   - Tag mutability: **Mutable** (allows overwriting `latest`)
   - Encryption: **AES-256**
   - **Scan on push: enabled** (each pushed image is automatically scanned for vulnerabilities)

### Step 3 — Image Build, Tag, and Push
Built a simple Docker image (nginx serving a static `index.html`, see [`docker/`](./docker)) and pushed multiple tagged versions to simulate real release cycles:

```bash
docker build -t <YOUR_ACCOUNT_ID>.dkr.ecr.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/my-app-repo:v1 .
docker push <YOUR_ACCOUNT_ID>.dkr.ecr.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/my-app-repo:v1

# repeat for v2, v3, v4 after modifying index.html

docker tag <YOUR_ACCOUNT_ID>.dkr.ecr.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/my-app-repo:v2 <YOUR_ACCOUNT_ID>.dkr.ecr.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/my-app-repo:latest
docker push <YOUR_ACCOUNT_ID>.dkr.ecr.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/my-app-repo:latest
```

Final image set in the repository: **v1, v2, v3, v4, latest** (latest points to the same image as v2 in this exercise — in practice it should always point to the newest release).

![ECR repository showing v1, v2, v3, v4, latest tags](./screenshots/01-ecr-images-list.png)

### Step 4 — Lifecycle Policy (Automated Cleanup)
Created a lifecycle rule directly on `my-app-repo`. Full policy: [`policies/lifecycle-policy.json`](./policies/lifecycle-policy.json)

```json
{
  "rules": [
    {
      "rulePriority": 1,
      "description": "keep last 3 images",
      "selection": {
        "tagStatus": "any",
        "countType": "imageCountMoreThan",
        "countNumber": 3
      },
      "action": {
        "type": "expire"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

**Logic:** Once the repository holds more than 3 images (across any tag), the oldest image beyond that count is automatically expired (deleted). ECR evaluates and applies this rule within 24 hours of a change; the **Preview rules** feature was used to validate the logic immediately without waiting.

![Lifecycle policy rule configured: keep last 3 images, expire the rest](./screenshots/02-lifecycle-policy-rule.png)

**Proof of cleanup logic (Preview Rules result):** `v1` — the oldest image — is correctly identified for expiry once the count exceeds 3.

![Preview rules result showing v1 marked for expiry](./screenshots/03-preview-cleanup-proof.png)

### Step 5 — Repository Policy (Access Control)
Attached a resource-based policy to the repository restricting who can interact with it. Full policy: [`policies/repository-policy.json`](./policies/repository-policy.json)

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "AllowPullForDevRole",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": { "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::<YOUR_ACCOUNT_ID>:role/EC2-ECR-Access-Role" },
      "Action": [
        "ecr:GetDownloadUrlForLayer",
        "ecr:BatchGetImage",
        "ecr:BatchCheckLayerAvailability"
      ]
    },
    {
      "Sid": "DenyAllOtherAccounts",
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Principal": "*",
      "Action": ["ecr:*"],
      "Condition": {
        "StringNotEquals": { "aws:PrincipalAccount": "<YOUR_ACCOUNT_ID>" }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

**Logic:**
- Only the `EC2-ECR-Access-Role` (the role attached to the Docker host) is explicitly granted pull permissions.
- Any principal outside this AWS account is explicitly denied all ECR actions on this repository — a defense-in-depth guardrail on top of standard IAM permissions.

![Repository policy showing Allow and Deny statements](./screenshots/04-repository-permissions.png)

---

## 4. Security Validation

Created a separate IAM user, `test-user`, with **no ECR permissions attached**, to simulate an unauthorized caller. Attempted to list repositories using that identity:

```bash
aws ecr describe-repositories --profile test-user
```

**Result — access correctly denied:**
```
An error occurred (AccessDeniedException) when calling the DescribeRepositories operation:
User: arn:aws:iam::<YOUR_ACCOUNT_ID>:user/test-user is not authorized to perform:
ecr:DescribeRepositories on resource: arn:aws:ecr:us-east-2:<YOUR_ACCOUNT_ID>:repository/*
because no identity-based policy allows the ecr:DescribeRepositories action
```

![Terminal showing AccessDeniedException for unauthorized test-user](./screenshots/05-unauthorized-access-denied.png)

This confirms IAM is correctly enforcing least-privilege access — an identity without an explicit ECR grant cannot read or interact with the registry, even though it belongs to the same AWS account.

---

## 5. Image Governance Explained

| Control | What it does | Why it matters |
|---|---|---|
| **Private repository** (vs. public Docker Hub) | Images are only reachable by authenticated, authorized identities within the account | Prevents accidental public exposure of proprietary code, configs, or secrets baked into an image |
| **Scan on push** | Every image is automatically scanned for known CVEs on push | Catches vulnerable base images/dependencies before they reach production |
| **IAM Role-based push/pull** (`EC2-ECR-Access-Role`) | Only the Docker build host's role can pull; no long-lived static credentials required | Removes the risk of leaked access keys; access is tied to the EC2 instance's identity |
| **Repository policy deny-by-default for other accounts** | Explicit deny for any principal outside the account | Defense-in-depth — even a misconfigured IAM policy elsewhere can't leak cross-account access |
| **Lifecycle policy (retain last 3, expire rest)** | Automatically deletes the oldest images once the count exceeds 3 | Controls storage cost and prevents unbounded growth, without manual cleanup |
| **Proper tagging (v1, v2, v3... + latest)** | Every build is versioned instead of overwriting a single tag | Enables rollback to a known-good version and gives the lifecycle policy something meaningful to act on |

---

## 6. Risk Mitigation Summary

| Risk (Before) | Mitigation (After) |
|---|---|
| Public repository exposure | Private ECR repository, account-scoped access only |
| No cleanup — unbounded storage growth/cost | Lifecycle policy expires images beyond the last 3 |
| Any IAM identity could interact with images | Repository policy + IAM role scoping restrict access to an explicit, audited role |
| No visibility into image vulnerabilities | Scan on push flags CVEs automatically |
| No version history / no rollback path | Proper `v1`, `v2`, `v3`... tagging preserves a rollback trail |

